ny, so the number of sunny lines in this collection is \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow we check that every point of \\(S\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\) because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=2\\), i.e. it lies on \\(L_{1}\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=3\\), i.e. it lies on \\(L_{2}\\).  \nIf \\(a+b=4\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=4\\), i.e. it lies on \\(L_{3}\\).\n\nThus each point of \\(S\\) is covered:\n\n| \\((a,b)\\) | \\(a+b\\) | covering line |\n|-----------|--------|----------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | 2      | \\(L_{1}\\)      |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | 3      | \\(L_{2}\\)      |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | 3      | \\(L_{2}\\)      |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | 4      | \\(L_{3}\\)      |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | 4      | \\(L_{3}\\)      |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | 4      | \\(L_{3}\\)      |\n\nThe three lines are distinct because their constants \\(2,3,4\\) differ.  \nAll required points are covered, and none of the lines is sunny.  \n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) there exists a configuration of three distinct lines with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=0\\text{ sunny lines exists; e.g. } \\{x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\}.}\n\\]"
