-(3,1):0,\\\\\n&(2,1)-(2,2):\\infty,\\qquad (1,3)-(3,1):-1,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):-1,\\qquad (3,1)-(2,2):-1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand similarly for the remaining symmetric pairs.\n\nHence the **only** pairs of points of \\(S_3\\) that lie on a sunny line are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The point \\((2,2)\\) cannot belong to a sunny line\n\nFor each \\(P\\in S_3\\setminus\\{(2,2)\\}\\) compute the slope of the line through \\(P\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(2,2):\\ 1,\\qquad (1,2)-(2,2):0,\\\\\n&(2,1)-(2,2):\\infty,\\qquad (1,3)-(2,2):-1,\\\\\n&(3,1)-(2,2):-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll these slopes are either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore **no** sunny line passes through \\((2,2)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Consequences for a covering by three lines\n\nWe must cover the six points of (1) with three distinct lines.  \nBecause \\((2,2)\\) cannot lie on a sunny line, at least one of the three lines must be non\u2011sunny; denote it \\(L_{\\text{ns}}\\).\n\nConsider the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nAny line through any two of them has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)), hence is non\u2011sunny. Consequently these three points cannot all be placed on a single sunny line; covering them requires **two** lines, at least one of which is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus, to cover all six points we need **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines:\n* one to contain \\((2,2)\\);\n* another to handle the triple \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nSince we are allowed only three lines in total, the number of sunny lines can be at most  \n\n\\[\n3-2 = 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nIf a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed, the remaining line would be the only non\u2011sunny line. As shown above, two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, a contradiction. Hence no configuration of three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_3\\) can have exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
