For any unordered pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset S_3\\) compute  \n\n\\[\nm(P,Q)=\\frac{y_Q-y_P}{\\,x_Q-x_P\\,}.\n\\]\n\nThe table below lists the slopes (the computation is elementary).\n\n| Pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\) | Slope \\(m(P,Q)\\) |\n|-------------------|-----------------|\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\)   | \\(-\\dfrac12\\)   |\n| \\((2,1),(1,3)\\)   | \\(-2\\)          |\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)   | \\(1\\) (non\u2011sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) (non\u2011sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)   | \\(0\\) (non\u2011sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(1,3)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,1),(3,1)\\)   | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(1,3)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)   | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)   | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(1,1)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(2,1)\\)   | \\(-2\\) (already listed) |\n| \\((1,3),(3,1)\\)   | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(2,2)\\)   | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(3,1)\\)   | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(1,1)\\)   | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(3,1)\\)   | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(1,3)\\)   | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(1,2)\\)   | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(2,1)\\)   | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(1,1)\\)   | \\(1\\) |\n\nThus the **only** pairs of points of \\(S_3\\) that lie on a sunny line are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other pairs give a line whose slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), i.e. a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The point \\((2,2)\\) cannot belong to a sunny line\n\nFor each \\(P\\in S_3\\setminus\\{(2,2)\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nm\\bigl((2,2),P\\bigr)\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1,1\\},\n\\]\n\nnone of which is allowed for a sunny line. Hence **no** sunny line passes through \\((2,2)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessity of at least two non\u2011sunny lines\n\nWe must cover the six points of (1) with exactly three distinct lines.  \nSince no three points of \\(S_3\\) are collinear (the only collinear triples would involve a point outside \\(S_3\\)), each line can contain at most two points. Consequently the three lines must partition \\(S_3\\) into three unordered pairs.\n\n* Because \\((2,2)\\) cannot be on a sunny line, the pair containing \\((2,2)\\) must be a non\u2011sunny line.\n* Consider the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\). Any pair among them has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each of the two pairs needed to cover these three points is non\u2011sunny. In particular, at least one additional non\u2011sunny line is required.\n\nThus **at least two** of the three lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n3-2 = 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nIf a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed, the remaining line would be the only non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the necessity of at least two non\u2011sunny lines proved above. Hence such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with exactly }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]"
