            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nBecause the line contains two integer points, \\(m\\) must be rational; write\n\\(m=p/r\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,r)=1\\) and \\(r\\ge2\\) (the case \\(r=1\\) would give an\ninteger slope, which would be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) – forbidden).  \n\nIf \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in L\\cap S_n\\) then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+q,\\qquad d=mc+q\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b-d=m(a-c)=\\frac p r (a-c).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a-c\\) is a multiple of \\(r\\).  In the triangular region\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the abscissa can increase at most by \\(n\\); therefore the number\nof distinct abscissae that differ by multiples of \\(r\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/r\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Consequently\n\\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  The bound is attained,\nfor instance by the line \\(y=x+1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting argument giving an upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximal column, row or anti‑diagonal has\n\\(n\\) points).  \n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nUsing Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =|S_n|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSolving (1) for \\(k\\) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn-2, & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more sunny lines than the right‑hand side of\n(2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions attaining the bound\n\n#### 3.1  Even \\(n=2m\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered except the single point \\((n,1)\\).\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we modify the configuration as\nfollows.\n\n* Choose the column \\(x=n-k\\) (which is still present among the vertical lines).\n* Remove the vertical line \\(V_{\\,n-k}\\).\n* Insert the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y-i = i\\,(x-(n-k))\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  Each line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((n-k,i)\\) (formerly covered by\n  \\(V_{\\,n-k}\\)) and also through the point \\((n,i+1)\\).  Hence the column\n  \\(x=n\\) becomes completely covered, and every other column remains covered by\n  the untouched vertical lines.\n\nThe family now consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n\\(k\\) are sunny (the lines in (3) have slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Therefore\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n#### 3.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\nBegin with the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nAll points are covered except the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) proceed analogously:\n\n* Choose the column \\(x=n-1-k\\) (present among the vertical lines).\n* Delete the vertical line \\(V_{\\,n-1-k}\\).\n* Insert the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y-i = i\\,(x-(n-1-k))\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  As before, \\(L_i\\) contains \\((n-1-k,i)\\) and \\((n,i+1)\\); consequently the\n  previously uncovered points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) become covered, while all\n  other columns stay covered by the remaining vertical lines.\n\nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every\n\\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe counting argument of Section 2 shows that no configuration can have more\nsunny lines than the numbers given in (2).  \nSections 3.1 and 3.2 provide explicit constructions that achieve every value\nup to those limits.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ if }n\\text{ is even},\\qquad\n0\\le k\\le n-2\\text{ if }n\\text{ is odd}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
