f the sunny family.*  \n   For an integer \\(c\\) let  \n   \\[\n   L_c:\\; y = x + c .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_c\\) is \\(1\\), therefore each \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Coverage Lemma (Lemma 3).*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Consider the set of sunny lines \\(\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) together with\n   the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Then every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (5)–(6).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 3.*  \n   Take an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+c\\) with\n   \\(c=b-a\\) and therefore belongs to the sunny line \\(L_c\\).  \n   Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  From the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+k)=n+1-a-k,\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to \\(a\\le n-k\\).  Hence the column \\(x=a\\) is one of the\n   vertical lines \\(V_j\\) with \\(j\\le n-k\\), and \\((a,b)\\) is covered by that\n   vertical line.  ∎\n\n   The family (5)–(6) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n   \\((n-k)\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. of \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n   Lemma 3 shows that they satisfy the covering requirement.\n   Consequently **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable for\n   any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) can be covered by the three sunny\n   lines\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=x-1,\n   \\]\n   giving \\(k=3\\).  Together with the construction of Lemma 3 (which yields\n   \\(k=0,1,2\\)) we obtain all four possibilities \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 provides the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) for all\n   \\(n\\ge4\\); Lemma 3 shows that this bound is tight.  The small case \\(n=3\\)\n   admits one extra value \\(k=3\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of\n   \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) contains two integer points, \\(m\\) is rational; write\n\\(m=p/r\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,r)=1\\) and \\(r\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap S_n\\) then\n\\(b_1-b_2=m(a_1-a_2)=\\frac p r (a_1-a_2)\\); hence \\(a_1-a_2\\) is a multiple of\n\\(r\\).  Consequently the abscissae of points of \\(L\\cap S_n\\) form an arithmetic\nprogression with step \\(r\\), so at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/r\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points can\nappear.  The case \\(r=1\\) gives the largest possible cardinality.\n\nWhen \\(r=1\\) the slope \\(m\\) is an integer different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\nIf \\(m\\neq1\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  The only integer slope that\ncan attain the larger value is \\(m=1\\); the line then has the form \\(y=x+c\\)\nwith integer \\(c\\).  Solving\n\\[\na\\ge\\max\\{1,1-c\\},\\qquad a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\n\\]\ngives exactly \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) admissible integers \\(a\\).  Hence\n\\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), and the bound is attained by the line\n\\(y=x\\) (for even \\(n\\)) or \\(y=x\\) together with the appropriate intercept\n(for odd \\(n\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nA non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Using Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  Even \\(n=2m\\)\n\nHere \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil=m\\).  Substituting in (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n4m^{2}-km\\ge 2m^{2}+m\\Longrightarrow k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nNow \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil=m+1\\).  Inequality (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)^{2}-km\\ge (2m+1)(m+1)\n\\Longrightarrow km\\le 2m^{2}+m .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\ge2\\), the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(nm=n(2m+1)\\); thus\n\\(k\\le n-1\\).  A more direct argument shows that the case \\(k=n\\) is\nimpossible: a sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal \\(\\{a+b=s\\}\\) in at most one\npoint, whereas the anti‑diagonal \\(s=n+1\\) contains \\(n\\) points while the\nanti‑diagonal \\(s=2\\) contains only one point.  If \\(k=n\\) all points were\ncovered, the anti‑diagonal \\(s=2\\) would have to contain a point from each line,\nwhich contradicts the “at most one” property.  Hence for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) we obtain for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection (see §4) shows that \\(k=3\\) is attainable,\nso the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) fails only in this smallest case.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 3.1  A family of sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\; y = x + c .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_c\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_c\\) is sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  Covering lemma\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 3.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }0\\le k\\le n-1. \\text{ The set }\n\\mathcal{L}= \\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{V_j:x=j,\\;1\\le j\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.*  If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\(b=a+c\\) with\n\\(c=b-a\\) and \\((a,b)\\in L_c\\).\n\n*Case 2.*  Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+k)=n+1-a-k,\n\\]\nhence \\(a\\le n-k\\).  Consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is present\nbecause \\(a\\le n-k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\). ∎\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the\n\\(L_c\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines, i.e. of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nBy Lemma 3 it satisfies the covering requirement.  Hence **for every**\n\\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration\nexists.\n\n#### 3.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=x-1\n\\]\n\ncover the three points, giving \\(k=3\\).  Together with the construction of\nLemma 3 (which yields \\(k=0,1,2\\)) we obtain all four possibilities\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor the exceptional size \\(n=3\\) the set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the complete description is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n0\\le k\\le n-1 , & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
