e n-1\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Define  \n\n* sunny lines \\(L_c:\\;y=x+c\\) for \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\);\n* vertical lines \\(V_j:\\;x=j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\).\n\nAll these lines are distinct; exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n\\(b=a+(b-a)\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n*Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a+k\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality using \\(k\\le n-1\\).  Hence the vertical line\n\\(V_a\\) (which belongs to our family) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family, and the\nfamily uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Consequently **every** integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The small case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=x-1\n\\]\n\ncover all three points, giving \\(k=3\\).  Together with the construction of\nSection 4 (which yields \\(k=0,1,2\\)) we obtain the complete set\n\\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the number of sunny lines in a configuration that\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), and each value in this interval is attainable.  For the\nexceptional size \\(n=3\\) the additional value \\(k=3\\) is possible.  Hence the\nset of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly t