 \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  Because the line contains two lattice points,\nits set of \\(x\\)-coordinates is an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\);\nhence at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\) (the right‑hand side of (1.1) is maximal\nwhen \\(q=1\\)).  Thus we may assume \\(q=1\\) and the slope is an integer\n\\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  The line has equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\).\nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it satisfy  \n\n\\[\na+ma+c\\le n+1\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad (1+m)a\\le n+1-c .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\ge0\\) then \\(1+m\\ge1\\) and the inequality gives  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{1+m}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nso at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) admissible integers \\(a\\).  \nIf \\(m\\le-2\\) then \\(1+m\\le-1\\); rewriting the inequality as\n\\((-(1+m))a\\ge c-(n+1)\\) yields the same bound.  Hence (1) holds for every\nsunny line. ∎\n\n\n\nMoreover the bound is attained exactly by the two families  \n\n\\[\nL^{(1)}_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z),\\qquad\nL^{(2)}_c:\\;y=-2x+(n+2)+c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z),\n\\]\n\neach of which contains \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  General upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) meets the triangle\n\\(\\{a\\ge0,b\\ge0,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.  \nIf a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, then\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   =|S_n|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSolving (2.1) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) is impossible  \n\nAssume that \\(n\\ge5\\) is odd and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\nBecause \\(|S_n|=n\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and each sunny line contains at most\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points (Lemma 1), every line must contain exactly\n\\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points; consequently each line belongs to one of the two\nextremal families described after Lemma 1.\n\nThere is **only one** line of the first family inside the triangle,\nnamely \\(y=x\\); likewise there is **only one** line of the second family,\nnamely \\(y=-2x+(n+2)\\).  Hence at most two distinct sunny lines can attain\nthe maximal size \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Since we would need \\(n\\ge5\\) distinct\nsunny lines, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.  Together with (2.2)\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=n\\ \\text{is possible only for }n=3 .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk_1=\\min\\!\\Bigl(k,\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr),\\qquad\nk_2=k-k_1 .\n\\]\n\nAll numbers below are integers.\n\n*Sunny lines.*\n\n* for each \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k_1-1\\) take  \n  \\[\n  L_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(\\text{slope }1);\n  \\]\n* for each \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k_2-1\\) take  \n  \\[\n  M_c:\\;y=-2x+(n+2)+c\\qquad(\\text{slope }-2).\n  \\]\n\nThese \\(k=k_1+k_2\\) lines are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*\n\nLet \\(r=n-k\\).  Choose the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n\\]\n\n(If \\(r=0\\) no non‑sunny line is needed.)\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and put \\(\\delta=b-a\\).\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(0\\le\\delta\\le k_1-1\\).  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_\\delta\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(-2\\le\\delta\\le -2+k_2-1\\).  \n  Write \\(\\delta=-2+t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k_2-1\\); then\n  \\(b=-2a+(n+2)+t\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_t\\).\n\n* **Case 3.** Otherwise \\(\\delta\\) is either \\(\\ge k_1\\) or \\(\\le -2-k_2\\).  \n\n  – If \\(\\delta\\ge k_1\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n    \\[\n    a\\le\\frac{n+1-k_1}{2}\\le r .\n    \\]\n    Hence the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n  – If \\(\\delta\\le -2-k_2\\) then \\(b\\le a-2-k_2\\).  Using again\n    \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n    \\[\n    a\\ge\\frac{n+3+k_2}{2}\\ge r+1 .\n    \\]\n    Consequently \\(b\\le r\\) and the vertical line \\(V_b\\) (which is among the\n    chosen vertical lines because \\(1\\le b\\le r\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn every case the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\nThus for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines exists, containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\).  \nThe construction of Section 4 yields configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \nIn addition, the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\npartition \\(S_3\\) into three disjoint pairs, giving a configuration with\n\\(k=3\\).  No configuration can have more than three lines, so the set of\nadmissible \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\) is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility results (Lemma 2, Lemma 3) with the explicit\nconstructions (Lemma 4 and the special treatment of \\(n=3\\)) we obtain the\ncomplete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n0\\le k\\le n-1, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]  \n\n∎"
