ge2\\) and (2) gives\n   \\(a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); together with\n   \\(a\\ge\\max\\{1,\\,\\lceil\\frac{1-c}{m}\\rceil\\}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(m\\le-2\\) we rewrite (2) as \\(- (1+|m|)a\\le n+1-c\\) and obtain the\n   same bound (3).  \n   Hence **every sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points**.\n   Moreover the bound is attained exactly by the two families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{y=x+c\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y=-2x+(n+2)+c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z)},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   each of which contains \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (upper bound for the number of sunny lines).**  \n   A non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Let a configuration use \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   Denoting by \\(s_i\\) and \\(t_j\\) the numbers of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the\n   \\(i\\)-th sunny and the \\(j\\)-th non‑sunny line, we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k}s_i+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\,n-k}t_j\\ge|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(s_i\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); by the remark above,\n   \\(t_j\\le n\\).  Substituting these bounds into (5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (the case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) is impossible).**  \n   Assume that \\(n\\ge5\\) is odd and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n   For odd \\(n\\) we have \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil=(n+1)/2\\); inserting \\(k=n\\) into\n   (6) shows that equality must hold in (6).  Consequently  \n\n   * every sunny line contains exactly \\((n+1)/2\\)