n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  For \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\;y=x+\\bigl(n-2k+1+t\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_t\\) has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny; the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts differ by \\(1\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   – If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n   – If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) then, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k+1\\;\\le\\;b-a\\;\\le\\;n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The integer \\(b-a\\) therefore belongs to the set \\(\\{\\,n-2k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\), which is exactly the set of intercepts occurring in (7).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of a unique line \\(S_t\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, and the construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  This proves the existence claim for all \\(k\\) satisfying (2).\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (the case \\(n=3\\)).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The following configurations show that every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): the three non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\).\n   * \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1\\).\n   * \\(k=2\\): the sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) together with the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\).\n   * \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad x+2y=5,\n     \\]\n\n     which are pairwise distinct and together cover all six points.\n\n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n**Conclusion**\n\n*Upper bound.*  Lemma 2 shows that for every \\(n\\ge4\\) one must have \\(k\\le n-1\\); for \\(n=3\\) we have \\(k\\le3\\).\n\n*Existence for small \\(k\\).*  Lemma 3 provides explicit configurations for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nLemma 4 settles the remaining values when \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus the problem is completely solved for the range \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (and for all \\(k\\) when \\(n=3\\)).  \nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor<k\\le n-1\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)) remains an open question.   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
