etailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor every sunny line \\(L\\) one has  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the slope of \\(L\\) as a reduced fraction \\(\\frac pq\\) with \\(q>0\\) and\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  All integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\).\nInside the interval \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) there are at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rceil\\) such values of \\(t\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).\n\nIf \\(q=1\\) the line has equation \\(y=px+c\\) with integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).\nThe condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na\\ge1,\\qquad pa+c\\ge1,\\qquad a+pa+c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(p>0\\) the last inequality gives  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{p+1}\\le\\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nand when \\(p<0\\) a symmetric estimate yields the same bound.\nThus in every case \\(|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and\ncontains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\begin{cases}\nn-1,&n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   &n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) meets \\(S_n\\) in at most\n\\(n\\) points (the lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\) attain the bound).  \nUsing Lemma 1 we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge|S_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\bigl(n-\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\bigr)}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n=2t\\) is even, the right–hand side equals \\(2t-1=n-1\\);  \nif \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd, it equals \\(2t+1=n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (parity obstruction)  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even, a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor even \\(n\\) we have \\(|S_n|=\\frac n2(n+1)\\), an odd integer.\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes an even number of points\n(\\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil=n/2\\) or \\(n/2+1\\) are both even).  \nThe union of \\(n\\) such lines therefore contains an even number of distinct\npoints, contradicting the odd size of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (reduction to a smaller triangle)  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the bound of Lemma 2 and put \\(m=n-k\\).\nChoose non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y=1,\\dots ,y=m_1,\\\\\n&x=1,\\dots ,x=m_2,\\\\\n&x+y=n+1,\\dots ,x+y=n+1-m_3,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad m_1+m_2+m_3=m .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m\\) lines are distinct.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is uncovered precisely when  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m_2+1,\\qquad b\\ge m_1+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1-m_3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a'=a-m_2,\\;b'=b-m_1\\).  From (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge1,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n+1-m_3-(m_1+m_2)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly the set \\(S_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (covering \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines)  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union\ncontains all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(k\\).*  \n\n*Base.*  \n\\(k=0\\) is trivial.  \n\\(k=1\\): the single sunny line \\(y=x\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\); together\nwith the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny) we cover \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for all integers \\(<k\\) (\\(k\\ge2\\)).\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+(k-2\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA direct check of the inequalities defining \\(S_k\\) shows that \\(L\\) meets\nexactly \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_k\\) and that the remaining points,\n after translating by \\((-1,0)\\), form the set \\(S_{k-1}\\).\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(S_{k-1}\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) distinct\nsunny lines; adding \\(L\\) yields a covering of \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAll the lines are distinct because each has a different intercept. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound of Lemma 2.\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\).  \nChoose non‑sunny lines as in Lemma 4 (any distribution of the three families\nwith total \\(m\\) lines).  \nBy Lemma 4 the points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by those \\(m\\) lines form the set\n\\(S_k\\).  Lemma 5 supplies \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_k\\).\nAltogether we have \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\n*Existence.*  \nLemma 6 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (2) there exists a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\nall points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Optimality.*  \nLemma 2 gives the universal upper bound (2); Lemma 3 proves that for even\n\\(n\\) the bound cannot be attained at \\(k=n\\).  Hence the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is precisely the one described in (2).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\;}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved. ∎"
