a+b\\le n+1-m_3 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a'=a-m_2,\\;b'=b-m_1\\).  From (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na',b'\\ge1,\\qquad a'+b'\\le n+1-m_3-(m_1+m_2)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly the set \\(S_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (covering \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines)  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union\ncontains all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(k\\).*  \n\n*Base.*  \n\\(k=0\\) is trivial.  \n\\(k=1\\): the single sunny line \\(y=x\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\); together\nwith the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny) we cover \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for all integers \\(<k\\) (\\(k\\ge2\\)).\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+(k-2\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA direct check of the inequalities defining \\(S_k\\) shows that \\(L\\) meets\nexactly \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_k\\) and that the remaining points,\n after translating by \\((-1,0)\\), form the set \\(S_{k-1}\\).\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(S_{k-1}\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) distinct\nsunny lines; adding \\(L\\) yields a covering of \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAll the lines are distinct because each has a different intercept. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound of Lemma 2.\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\).  \nChoose non‑sunny lines as in Lemma 4 (any distribution of the three families\nwith total \\(m\\) lines).  \nBy Lemma 4 the points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by those \\(m\\) lines form the set\n\\(S_k\\).  Lemma 5 supplies \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_k\\).\nAltogether we have \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them. ∎\n\n\n\n---