ubtract \\(1\\) from each coordinate; the\n        resulting point belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is covered by the original\n        configuration.\n\n     Hence we obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n     Because \\(k\\) could be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\), the new\n     configuration supplies all integers \\(k'\\) with  \n\n     \\[\n     k' = k+1\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}= \\{0,1,\\dots ,(n+2)-1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Adding also the possibility \\(k'=0\\) (take only the two non\u2011sunny lines\n     \\(y=1,\\;x=1\\) together with a suitable configuration for the remaining\n     \\(n\\) points) shows that every \\(k'\\le n+2\\) is attainable when\n     \\(n+2\\) is odd.\n\n   - **Even\u2011to\u2011odd transition** (\\(n\\) even, \\(n+2\\) odd).  \n     Begin with a configuration for \\(n\\) having \\(k\\) sunny lines\n     (\\(k\\le n-1\\)).  \n     Add the sunny line \\(y=x\\) and the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\) as in the\n     odd\u2011to\u2011odd case.  \n     The same covering argument works, giving a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with\n     \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. Since \\(k\\) ranges up to \\(n-1\\), the new number\n     \\(k+1\\) ranges up to \\(n\\), i.e. up to \\((n+2)-2\\).  Together with the\n     possibility \\(k'=0\\) we obtain all admissible values for the odd integer\n     \\(n+2\\).\n\n   - **Odd\u2011to\u2011even transition** (\\(n\\) odd, \\(n+2\\) even).  \n     Start from a configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n     (\\(k\\le n\\)).  \n     Add the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).  \n     The covering argument identical to the even\u2011to\u2011even case shows that we\n     obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny\n     lines.  Because \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(n+2\\) is even, the bound (3) for the even\n     case (\\(k\\le n+1\\)) is respected, and all integers \\(k\\le n+1\\) are\n     realised.\n\n   The four transitions together prove, by induction on \\(n\\), that for every\n   integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 gives the necessary condition (3); Lemma\u202f3 shows that the same\n   condition is sufficient.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n   exactly the one described in (3).\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\),\n\\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Maximum points on a sunny line)\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac pq\\) with integers\n\\(p,q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nAll integer points on the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is one integer point on the line.\nFor two consecutive points the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=(q+p).\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\((q,p)\\neq(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\); consequently\n\\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\).  \nInside the strip \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) can assume at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) different values, hence a sunny\nline meets at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the bound is\nattained by the three lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\)).  \nIf a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, then\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\ge N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M\\) from (1) and solve (2) for \\(k\\).  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even, \\(M=\\dfrac{n}{2}\\) and (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac n2+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, \\(M=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) and (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n(3) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base cases \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe four possibilities are exhibited below (the line \\(y=x\\) is sunny,\nthe others are not).\n\n| \\(k\\) | lines |\n|------|-------|\n|0| \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\) |\n|1| \\(y=x,\\;y=1,\\;x=1\\) |\n|2| \\(y=x,\\;y=2,\\;x=1\\) |\n|3| \\(y=x,\\;y=x+2,\\;y=x+4\\) (possible because \\(3\\) is odd) |\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nWe construct configurations for \\(n+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.  From an even \\(n\\) to the even integer \\(n+2\\)\n\nLet a configuration for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines be given\n(\\(k\\le n-1\\) by the induction hypothesis).  \nAdd the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+2}\\) falls into one of the following classes:\n\n* **(i)** \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\): the point lies on one of the lines (4);\n* **(ii)** \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\): subtract \\(1\\) from each coordinate; the\n  resulting point belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is covered by the original\n  configuration.\n\nThus we obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines.  Because \\(k\\le n-1=n+2-3\\), all admissible values for the even\ninteger \\(n+2\\) are realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.  From an odd \\(n\\) to the odd integer \\(n+2\\)\n\nStart with a configuration for \\(n\\) having \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(\\(k\\le n\\) by the induction hypothesis).  \nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+2}\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n* **(i)** \\(b=1\\): the point lies on the added horizontal line;\n* **(ii)** \\(a=b\\): the point lies on the added line \\(L\\);\n* **(iii)** \\(a\\neq b\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\): after subtracting \\(1\\) from each\n  coordinate we obtain a point of \\(S_n\\), which is covered by the original\n  configuration.\n\nConsequently we obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\nSince the induction hypothesis supplies all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\nthe new construction yields all integers  \n\n\\[\nk' = k+1\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}= \\{0,1,\\dots ,(n+2)-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTogether with the trivial case \\(k'=0\\) (use only the two non\u2011sunny lines\n\\(y=1,\\;x=1\\) together with a suitable configuration for the remaining\n\\(n\\) points) we obtain every admissible \\(k'\\) for the odd integer \\(n+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.  From an even \\(n\\) to the odd integer \\(n+2\\)\n\nTake a configuration for the even \\(n\\) with \\(k\\le n-1\\) sunny lines.\nAdd the sunny line \\(y=x\\) and the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\) as in the previous\ncase.  The same covering argument shows that we obtain a configuration for\n\\(n+2\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  Because \\(k\\) ranges up to \\(n-1\\), the new\nnumber \\(k+1\\) ranges up to \\(n\\), i.e. up to \\((n+2)-2\\).  Adding the\npossibility \\(k'=0\\) (two non\u2011sunny lines plus a suitable configuration for the\nremaining points) yields all admissible values for the odd integer \\(n+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.  From an odd \\(n\\) to the even integer \\(n+2\\)\n\nBegin with a configuration for the odd \\(n\\) having \\(k\\le n\\) sunny lines.\nAdd the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\).  The covering argument\nidentical to the even\u2011to\u2011even case shows that we obtain a configuration for\n\\(n+2\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.  Since \\(k\\le n\\) and the\nupper bound for an even integer \\(n+2\\) is \\(n+1\\) (see (3)), all admissible\nvalues are realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe four transitions together prove, by induction on \\(n\\), that for every\ninteger \\(k\\) satisfying (3) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that no configuration can have more sunny lines than the\nnumbers given in (3).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (3) a configuration\nexists.  Hence the condition (3) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
