            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet a sunny line have reduced direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is an integer point on the line, then all integer points on\nthe line are  \n\n\\[\n(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nHence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(\\Delta=p+q\\) when we move from one integer\npoint to the next.  Because \\((q,p)\\) is reduced and not equal to\n\\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\), we have \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\).  Inside the strip\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) can take at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) different values; consequently a\nsunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the bound is attained by the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nUsing Lemma 1 and (2),\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\ge N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the value of \\(M\\) from (1) and solve (3) for \\(k\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(M=n/2\\); (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac n2+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, \\(M=(n+1)/2\\); (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\Longrightarrow k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nWe treat three situations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three non‑sunny lines (2) with \\(n=3\\), i.e.  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4,\n  \\]\n\n  cover all six points.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) together with the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;x=1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;( (1,2),(3,1) ),\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\\;( (1,3),(2,1) )\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, each contains exactly two points of \\(S_3\\), and together\n  they cover the whole set.  \n\n*The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.*  \nIf a non‑sunny line is chosen among the three lines (2), the three points of\n\\(S_3\\) not lying on it are pairwise such that any line through two of them\nhas slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny).  Consequently two\nsunny lines cannot cover the remaining three points.  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the\nadmissible values are exactly \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inductive constructions for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4)\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\ncovering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe induction hypothesis supplies a configuration for some \\(n\\ge3\\).\nWe describe how to obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (i) Adding two non‑sunny lines (keeps the number of sunny lines)\n\nAdd the lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad x=1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are **new**, because every line occurring in the induction\nhypothesis lies completely inside the rectangle \\(\\{1\\le a,b\\le n\\}\\); the\nlines (5) intersect the rectangle only on its left and bottom edges, which are\nnot used by any line of the hypothesis (otherwise that line would already be\nnon‑sunny and equal to one of (5), contradicting the distinctness of the\nhypothesis).  \n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+2}\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n* \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\): the point lies on one of the lines (5);\n* \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\): the point \\((a-1,b-1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is covered\n  by the original configuration.\n\nThus the new family of \\(n+2\\) lines covers all points of \\(S_{n+2}\\) and\ncontains exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines as the original family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (ii) Adding one sunny and one non‑sunny line (increases the number of sunny lines by 1)\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nand the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\).  As before, \\(L\\) and \\(y=1\\) are new because\ntheir intersection with the rectangle \\(\\{1\\le a,b\\le n\\}\\) lies on the\nboundary \\(a=b\\) or \\(b=1\\), none of which is used by the induction\nconfiguration (otherwise a line of the configuration would be non‑sunny and\ncoincide with one of the added lines).  \n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+2}\\) we distinguish:\n\n* \\(b=1\\): the point lies on the added horizontal line;\n* \\(a=b\\): the point lies on the added diagonal line (6);\n* otherwise \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\): the point \\((a-1,b-1)\\) belongs to\n  \\(S_n\\) and is covered by the original configuration.\n\nHence we obtain a configuration for \\(n+2\\) with **\\(k+1\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### (iii) Obtaining all admissible values\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nStarting from the base case \\(n=4\\) (which we construct explicitly below),\nrepeatedly apply construction (i) to keep the number of sunny lines unchanged.\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (the bound (4) for even\n\\(n\\)) is realised.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \nBegin with the explicit configuration for \\(n=5\\) given in the next subsection.\nFrom there, alternating constructions (i) and (ii) allows us to increase the\nsize of the set by two while either preserving \\(k\\) (using (i)) or increasing\n\\(k\\) by one (using (ii)).  Consequently we can realise **all** integers\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), i.e. the whole range prescribed by (4) for odd\n\\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Explicit configurations for the smallest even and odd sizes\n\n*Even \\(n=4\\).*  \nTake the three non‑sunny lines (2) with \\(n=4\\) and one sunny line\n\\(y=x\\).  The four lines are distinct, contain exactly one sunny line, and\ncover all points of \\(S_4\\).  By adding further non‑sunny lines as in (i) we\nobtain configurations with any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n*Odd \\(n=5\\).*  \nConsider the following five lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_1:\\;y=1, &&\\text{(non‑sunny)}\\\\\n&L_2:\\;x=1, &&\\text{(non‑sunny)}\\\\\n&L_3:\\;x+y=6, &&\\text{(non‑sunny)}\\\\\n&L_4:\\;y=x,   &&\\text{(sunny)}\\\\\n&L_5:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72\\;(\\text{through }(1,3),(5,1)), &&\\text{(sunny)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(S_5\\) lies on at least one of\nthese five lines.  The configuration contains exactly two sunny lines.\nApplying constructions (i) and (ii) repeatedly yields configurations for any\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma 2 gives the necessary condition (4).  \nLemma 3 together with the explicit base configurations for \\(n=3,4,5\\) shows that\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the bound (4) is also sufficient, and the special\nanalysis of \\(n=3\\) shows that the only admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\ge4\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\n&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\ge5\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n=3 .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
